 "abstract": "This chapter explores the ways in which middle-class masculine identities are constructed through the curriculum and the daily routines of school, including sport and after-school club activities. This socialization takes place through participation in traditionally masculine sports like rugby, baseball, and mountaineering, as well as through gendered attitudes to risk-taking. The ending of the age of empire and the decline of the ideology of militarism in both nations brought about a change to the nature of orthodox masculine values in the years following World War II. The experiences of boys who do not fit orthodox images of heterosexual masculinity will be discussed. Groups campaigning for the rights of sexual minorities in the UK were successful in the 1980s and 1990s in overturning discriminatory laws and attitudes. However, in Japan there has mostly been silence on these topics, meaning that there is very little support for school students or teachers who are members of these minorities."
}